{"1203":"Sewer systems in newly established subdivisions may be connected to the city sewage works provided the following conditions are met:\nThe collecting system within the subdivision and the connecting pipes to the public sewer are designed by a registered professional engineer of this state and approved by all applicable agencies.\nAn application for approval of the proposed works shall be submitted to the city prior to start of construction.\nThe volume of the collected wastes from the proposed system shall not cause any part of the sewage works to become overloaded.\nAll costs applicable to the proposed works shall be the responsibility of the applicant.\nApproval of the completed sewage works must be obtained from the superintendent before the city shall assume responsibility of ownership.\nAll proposed extensions shall be public extensions and shall be approved based on that extension's conformance to the city's standards and specifications as promulgated in this article. Prior to acceptance, all as-built plans, property rights-of-way and easements, and certifications of the owner's engineer shall be filed with the city for formal acceptance.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, § 10.3)","1206":"All property directly connected to the sewer system and served by sewage works shall be subject to fees in support of the construction, extension, and improvement of the public sanitary sewers, to be levied and collected in accordance with this division. The cost of principal and interest, either bonded or otherwise, shall be borne by those users connected to the system.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, § 11.1)","1207":"All new users shall be required to pay a connection fee or a buy-in assessment at a rate determined to be proportionate to existing user costs of capitalization of the facilities.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, § 11.1)","1208":"The annual operating and maintenance cost of the sewer system shall be paid by the users of the system in proportion to their use. Charges for use of the system shall be based upon volume of wastewater discharged to the sewage works.\nThe city shall establish a user rate in accordance with appropriate federal and state rules and regulations pertaining to the costs associated to the use of the sewer by residential, commercial, and individual users.\nThe city shall, in establishing the rates referred to in this division, make specific reference to the sewer use rate structure in force at the time of any connection. The sewer use rate structure shall incorporate the requirements of 40 CFR 35.935-13.\nRates provided for within this article shall be established by the mayor and council and may be changed from time to time as required.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, §§ 11.2—11.5)","1209":"Users intending to connect to the public sewer shall pay an initial hookup charge which shall be established by the city and may be changed from time to time as required. These hookup charges may be used for either pre-existing, current or future capital improvements. Hookup charges shall be payable in full prior to the start of construction for all additions, improvements to, and/or enlargements of existing buildings, or upon the start of the construction or installation of non-building or accessory structures directly and indirectly connected to the public sewer.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, §§ 11.6, 11.7)","1210":"All sums payable to the city under this division shall be secured by a lien on all real property and improvements intended to be served by the connection for which the payment is due in the manner and to the same effect as taxes are a lien upon real estate and shall be further secured by a lien on any commercial equipment, the ordinary use and operation of which generates wastewater, and a lien on any and all furnishings, fixtures and equipment used in connection with the use and operation of such commercial equipment, to the same effect as taxes are a lien upon personal property.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, § 11.8)","1213":"The superintendent and other duly authorized employees of the city bearing proper credentials and identification shall be permitted to enter all properties for the purpose of inspection, observation, measurement, sampling and testing in accordance with the provisions of this article.\nWhile performing necessary work on private properties, the superintendent or duly authorized employees of the city shall observe all safety rules applicable to the premises established by the property owner.\nThe superintendent and other duly authorized employees of the city shall be permitted to enter all private properties through which the city holds an easement for the purposes of, but not limited to, inspection, observation, measurement, sampling, repair, and maintenance of any portion of the sewage works lying within said easement. All entry and subsequent work, if any, on said easement, shall be done in full accordance with the terms of the easement pertaining to the private property involved.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, art. 8)","1214":"Violations of this article shall be a misdemeanor punishable as provided in section 1-13. Each day of continuing violation shall be considered a separate offense. Any person violating any of the provisions of this article shall become liable to the city for any expense, loss, or damage occasioned by the city as a result of said violation.\nIn the event of violation of this article, the health officer or the city may verbally instruct the property owner as to the necessary corrective action. If the property owner fails to carry out verbal instructions in a timely manner or if a serious violation or hazard to public health exists, the health officer or the city may issue to the owner a written order stating the nature of the violation, the corrective action and the time limit for completing the corrective action. The written order shall be mailed via certified U.S. mail to the property owner's last known address and additionally a copy of the written order shall be hand-delivered to the subject building or property. If no person is available to accept the order at the building or property, then it shall be posted at the primary entrance to the building or property.\nFailure to comply with any written order duly issued by the health officer or the city pursuant to this article will constitute a separate misdemeanor and upon conviction thereof shall be punishable as provided by the laws of the state; provided further that compliance with this article is required notwithstanding the fact that a written order might not have been issued.\nThe violation of any provisions of this article, as now existing or as may be hereafter amended, may be enjoined by instituting appropriate proceedings for injunction in the courts of competent jurisdiction in this state. Any public nuisance, which is injurious to the public health, safety, or comfort, may be abated by instituting appropriate proceedings for injunction in the court of competent jurisdiction in this state. Such actions may be maintained notwithstanding the fact that such violation also constitutes a crime, and notwithstanding that other adequate remedies at law exist. Such actions may be instituted in the name of the city.\nUpon the receipt of a notice of violation of this article or an order of the city requiring an act or thing to be done or to cease, the owner or owners of any premises then in question may, in writing, demand a hearing before the city council to present evidence challenging the validity of the city order. The owner may appear in person or by agent with or without an attorney. Said hearing demand must be filed with the city clerk within five days from receipt of the order being challenged. Upon receipt of a proper written demand for a hearing, the city will set a date, time, and place for said hearing to be not less than 30 days from the date of filing said demand. The city's decision at said hearing will be a final decision subject to direct appeal to the county superior court or other court of competent jurisdiction.\nEvidence before the city of any hearing conducted shall be admitted in accordance with the rules of evidence of the superior courts of the state; provided, however, the city may take official notice of any order, rule, regulation, or any other document, record, or entry contained in its official record or minutes for evidentiary purposes.\nFor the purposes of this article, the decisions of the city will prevail in any instance in which there is a conflict between it and the health officer on any issue of sewage sanitation, or the lack of it, and its effect on human health or wellbeing.\n(Ord. of 7-7-2008, art. 9)","1215":"This table gives the location within this Code of those sections of the Compiled Ordinances of 1998 that are included herein. Sections of the Compiled Ordinances of 1998 not listed herein have been omitted as repealed, superseded, obsolete or not of a general and permanent nature. For the location of ordinances adopted subsequent thereto, see the table immediately following this table.\nComp. Ords. 1998 Section | Section this Code\n1-1-1—1-1-3 | 1-1—1-3\n1-1-4 | 1-11\n1-1-5 | 1-13\n1-1-6 | 1-8\n1-1-7 | 1-12\n2-1-1 | 2-21\n2-1-11—2-1-17 | 2-84—2-90\n2-2-1 | 14-19\n2-2-2 | 14-21\n2-2-3, 2-2-4 | 14-46, 14-47\n2-2-5 | 14-20\n2-4-1 | 2-154\n2-4-2 | 2-182\n2-4-3 | 2-203\n2-5-1 | 2-286\n2-5-2, 2-5-3 | 2-289, 2-290\n2-5-11—2-5-13 | 44-19—44-21\n2-5-21 | 10-1\n2-5-30 | 10-158\n2-5-41, 2-5-42 | 4-74, 4-75\n3-1-1, 3-1-2 | 28-23, 28-24\n3-2-1 | 20-25\n3-2-2 | 20-54\n3-2-3 | 20-27\n3-2-4 | 20-55\n3-2-21 | 20-86\n3-2-22(a)—3-2-22(d) | 20-21—20-24\n3-2-22(e) | 20-26\n3-2-24—3-2-26 | 20-87—20-89\n3-2-27 | 20-1\n3-3-1 | 16-1\n4-1-1—4-1-5 | 40-31—40-35\n4-1-6, 4-1-7 | 40-2, 40-3\n4-1-8 | 40-6\n4-1-9 | 40-1\n4-2-1 | 38-1\n4-2-2 | 38-4\n4-2-3(a) | 38-5\n4-2-3(b), 4-2-3(c) | 38-36\n4-2-4 | 38-29\n4-2-5(a)—4-2-5(c) | 38-62—38-64\n4-2-5(d) | 38-66\n4-2-5(e) | 38-65\n4-2-6 | 38-61\n4-2-7, 4-2-8 | 38-34, 38-35\n4-2-9(a) | 38-3\n4-2-9(c) | 38